581:    Given $G$, $W$, $U_1$ and $U_2$ as stated, we choose $K$ to be sufficiently large. Note that $\abs{U_1\cup U_2}\ge 2D$ and $\abs{V(F_3)\cup V(F_4)}=2D$. By \eqref{unit5}, we have $1/4\cdot\ep(2D)\cdot 2D\ge 1/4\cdot\ep(n)\cdot 2D\ge D/\log^3\frac{n}{\kappa^2}\ge \abs{W}$. Then by Lemma \ref{lem: small-diameter-lemma}, there is a shortest path $P'\subseteq G-W$ from $U_1\cup U_2$ to $V(F_3)\cup V(F_4)$ of length at most $m$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that $P'$ goes from some $v_1\in U_1$ to $v'_3\in V(F_3)$. As $F_3$ is a $(D,m)$-expansion of $v_3$, there is a $v_1,v_3$-path $P$ of length at most $2m$ in $U_1\cup P'\cup F_3$.
