The [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.12012) proves extremal existence, not hardness for inverse-planted instances. The natural length-2 planted family failed H:

- Common-neighbor matching found valid, mostly unrelated subdivisions on 20/20 dense instances.
- Sparse variants leaked planted branch vertices through degree statistics.
- Track B had no solver-visible compact route distinct from ordinary matching.

The complete theorem audit, measurements, density sweep, and mechanical-versus-compact cost comparison are in [REJECTED.md](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2204.12012/REJECTED.md).
